# --- PRIVACY: real patient source material (never committed) ---
#
# The reference-patient photographs live in the working tree, NOT outside the repo as was
# assumed. They are photographs of a real person's medical records. These rules are
# defensive and deliberately broad: the cost of a false positive is a developer renaming an
# asset, the cost of a false negative is publishing someone's medical records to GitHub.
#
# Enforced by tests/test_privacy.py, which fails the build if any of them becomes tracked.
# Source medical photographs. NO TRAILING SLASH — it used to be `*stroke report*/`, which
# matches directories only, so `real stroke report.zip` (an archive of all 22 photographs)
# was ignored solely by the `*.zip` build-artifact rule above. Narrow or remove that rule
# and somebody's hospital records become stageable. Two independent rules now cover it.
*stroke report*
*stroke report*.zip
*.jpeg
*.jpg

# Local-only list of real identifiers used by tests/test_privacy.py. Never committed.
.privacy-denylist

# Local database backups taken before each migration. They contain seeded demo data and
# are binary snapshots of whatever the dev DB held at the time — never publish them.
.backups/

# Throwaway probes
frontend/scratch/

# Playwright MCP session artefacts + root-level screenshots
.playwright-mcp/
/*.png

# Throwaway browser-sweep scripts written next to the app during debugging.
frontend/*.mjs

# The generated knowledge graph. NEVER COMMIT THIS (INV-11).
#
# It indexes every tracked source file, and that includes `backend/tests/test_privacy.py`,
# which necessarily quotes the identifier patterns it hunts for. Committing the graph would
# therefore trip INV-11's own text scan on the scanner's bait. It is a ~5.5 MB artefact that
# regenerates in about a minute with `graphify update .`, so there is nothing to weigh
# against the invariant.
#
# It was untracked but NOT ignored: `git check-ignore graphify-out/` returned 0, which reads
# like a pass and is not one - the files inside were never matched, and `git add -A` would
# have staged 163 of them.
graphify-out/

# Video, unconditionally (INV-11).
#
# Three recordings sat untracked in the repo root - two screen captures and one named after
# a person. Untracked is not ignored: `git add -A` would have staged all three, and the
# preflight's media checks look for IMAGE extensions, so nothing downstream would have
# caught it. A screen recording of this product shows patient-facing screens by definition,
# and a file named after someone is the exact thing INV-11 exists to keep out.
#
# No exception list here on purpose. Demo footage belongs in the deck, not the repository.
*.mp4
*.mov
*.avi
*.mkv
*.webm
